  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Aaron Civale gave them four clean innings. The bullpen finished what he started.",
    "what_this_means": "They trailed after the fourth inning but answered with four runs in the fifth. The kind of gut-check response that wins tight games. Jeff McNeil went 2-for-3 and converted runners into actual runs. Henry Bolte added another hit with an RBI. Ten hits total, nine strikeouts. The math said close. The execution said controlled. Kuhnel, Suarez, Medina, and Harris combined for four shutout innings. Each one a strikeout. This is how you protect a one-run lead in the late innings: clean work from reliable arms, no room for San Francisco to breathe.",
    "what_to_watch": "The margin is starting to feel real. After the loss yesterday, they could have folded. Instead, they recaptured a lead they had held and didn't give it back. That's not a slump correcting itself. That's a team knowing how to finish when it matters. One win doesn't trend a season, but three straight one-run wins is a shape you notice. Division pressure doesn't ease with single games. It eases with patterns. They have one.",
